description:
crate-jmap-sharing-server/src/lib.rs:201-213

    #[derive(Clone)]
    pub struct MockBackend {
        state: Arc<Mutex<HashMap<String, AccountState>>>,
    }

    impl MockBackend {
        pub fn new() -> Self { ... }
    }

The struct exposes a public pub fn new() -> Self constructor but does not implement Default. clippy::new_without_default (a default-on style lint) flags this exact shape. The fix is mechanical:

    #[derive(Clone, Default)]
    pub struct MockBackend { ... }

    impl MockBackend {
        pub fn new() -> Self { Self::default() }
    }

The Inner field state: Arc<Mutex<HashMap<...>>> is Default-derivable (Arc<T: Default>, Mutex<T: Default>, HashMap<K, V> are all Default). The compiler-generated Default impl matches the existing new() body exactly.

Why this matters: Default is part of the Rust ecosystem convention for empty-constructible types — code generic over Default (e.g. Option<MockBackend>::unwrap_or_default(), HashMap::entry().or_default()) gets MockBackend for free. Without it the type is awkwardly outside the standard convention.

Sibling-scope: the sibling MockBackend types in jmap-tasks-server, jmap-calendars-server, jmap-contacts-server, jmap-chat-server have the same shape. Workspace-wide propagation in one sweep.

Note: clippy doesn't fire on this in the current workspace lint config — the type lives inside #[cfg(test)] mod test_support, and the new_without_default lint may be ignored under cfg(test). It's still a real idiom miss: this is a pub struct in a pub-tested module, and the Default impl costs zero to add.

The canonical MemoryBackend at memory.rs:151 already has #[derive(Clone, Default)] paired with pub fn new() -> Self { Self::default() } — the in-tree precedent for the right shape exists; MockBackend just didn't copy it.
